IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `cm64-nouns`

-- Row il-64. THE TWO ITEMS (I1 and I2), as typed values, each with a sound control beside it.
-- A reference is encoded as TWO descriptions: what the citing words say they point at, and what the target is, with a count of what is named and what the target has. Where they differ, the citation does not point at what it says.
-- DEFECT I1: s 21ב(ג) cites s 6ל(א); the number 6ל was re-marked 21ג by the Act in Sefer HaChukim 1807, and the consolidation prints a note under the old number.
-- DEFECT I2: s 6ח4(ב) cites s 6יב2(1); the Act in Sefer HaChukim 2985 (Amendment 76), s 26, repeals ss 6יב1 to 6יב4, and the consolidation prints a note that s 6יב2 is repealed. The failing assertions are in cm64-tests-xrefs-red.l4.
-- CONTROLS: for I1, the three subsections that the same sentence of s 21ב(ג) cites after the semicolon; for I2, the defined term of s 6ח4(ב), which s 6ח4(ג) defines.
-- LIMBS. Limb (a) is the question whether the target is as the citing words say; limb (b) is the question whether the citing words read so in the instrument that made them.
-- Each limb says where it was read from (see `Where a reading comes from` in cm64-nouns.l4). The Hebrew Wikisource text is not official, and anything resting only on it is CONSOLIDATION-ONLY.
-- This row records what each instrument prints. It does not say that the Law or anyone is wrong, and it does not say what a court would do with the words.
-- The printed pages show a section number such as 6ח4 with its last digit as a small raised numeral; this file types it as a plain digit, as the consolidation does.

§ `I1: the citation of s 6ל(א) in s 21ב(ג)`

